==English==

===Etymology===
From {{uder|en|la|viridis}}, from the verb {{m|la|vireō|t=be verdant, sprout}}.
{{root|en|ine-pro|*(s)ker-|id=turn|*weys-}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{rhymes|en|ɪdiən|s=4}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# A [[bluish]]-[[green]] [[pigment]] made from [[chromium sesquioxide]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1890|author=w:Arthur Herbert Church|title=The Chemistry of Paints and Painting|location=London|publisher=Seeley & Co.|edition=3rd|year_published=1901|chapter=16|page=195|url=https://catalog.hathitrust.org/Record/100242113
|passage=It would be difficult to exaggerate the value of this addition to the artist’s palette. The colour of '''viridian''' is a very deep bluish green of great purity and transparency. It furnishes, with [[aureolin]] on the one hand and with [[ultramarine]] on the other, an immense number of beautiful hues, adapted to represent the colours of vegetation and of water.}}
#* '''1995''', {{w|Michael Chabon}}, ''{{w|Wonder Boys}}'', Ch.1, at p.16:
#*: Then Miss Sloviak emerged from the ladies' room, hair reestablished, cheeks rouged, eyelids freshly painted a soft '''viridian''', smelling of what I recognised as Cristalle, the fragrance worn both by my wife, Emily, and also by my lover, Sara Gaskell.
# A bluish-green color.
#: {{color panel|40826D}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# Of a bluish [[green]] colour.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1928|author=w:Humbert Wolfe|chapter=Spring|title=This Blind Rose|location=London|publisher=Victor Gollancz|page=47|url=https://catalog.hathitrust.org/Record/006119975
|passage=spring sweeps the wood’s cathedral nave<br>with the green fury of a wave,<br>till oak and elm and beech and ash<br>in one '''viridian''' [[comber]] crash,<br>while at their feet red vetches shine,<br>sharp, and cold, and coralline.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1937|author=w:Robert Byron|title=w:The Road to Oxiana|chapter=[[w:Bamyan|Bamian]]|url=http://www.gutenberg.net.au/ebooks14/1403301h.html
|passage=We walked out on to a balcony, looking down on the bright green fields, the grey-blue river lined with '''viridian''' poplars, and the red earth paths where the peasants were driving their animals {{...}}}}

